.\" vim: tw=75
.TH CRC "1" "May 2016" "IndiMail" "General Commands"

.SH NAME
.PP
crc \- checksum files
.PP
crcdiff \- compare two crc lists and report the difference

.SH SYNOPSYS
.PP
\fBcrc\fR [-wdlv] [-i \fIinitial_crc\fR] [-t \fItag\fR] file ...
.PP
\fBcrcdiff\fR [-Cdi] [-c \fIcritical_list\fR] \fIcrclist\fR \fIcrclist\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
For each file, \fBcrc\fR calculates and prints a POSIX 32-bit CRC in
hexadecimal. With the -v option it additionally prints file permissions,
file owner, group and the file name. It is typically used to validate files
transferred between different systems, and is useful in detecting subtle
disk corruption. \fBcrc\fR uses a checksum compatible with the DOS version
of \fBcrc\fR, the 32 bit CRC used by PKZIP version 0.9. as well as the
"crc" command in ZCOMM and Professional-YAM (high reliability
communications programs).

The 32-bit CRC used is the frame check sequence in ADCCP (ANSI X3.66, also
known as FIPS PUB 71 and FED-STD-1003, the U.S. version of CCITT's X.25
link-level protocol).

\fBcrcdiff\fR compares two crc lists and reports the differences.

.EX
  changed    - crc changed without date change 
  replaced   - crc + date changed
  permiss    - permissions changed
  own/grp    - owner or group changed
  deleted    - 
  added      - Print the info for the new file
  WARNING L1 - Changes to setuid, setgid files
  WARNING L2 - Changes to file in \fIcritical_list\fR
.EE
.PP
\fBcrcdiff\fR reads the two crc lists into memory once and is indexed with
hash tables. The hash function is djb2 (h = h*33 + c, starting at 5381). So
the run time of \fBcrcdiff\fR is proportional to the size of the two files.

To generate \fIcrclist\fR you can use

.EX
 find / -print | sort | xargs crc -v > crc_file
.EE

or

.EX
 crc -Rv -f list_file -o crc_file
.EE

to generate a crc list. See the \fBEXAMPLES\fR section for more usage
options.

.SH CRC OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Eliminate broken links. If passed, \fBcrc\fR will skip symbolic links that
points to non-existing files

.TP
.B \-s
Follow symlinks. This option makes \fBcrc\fR call stat(2) after a lstat(2).
Along with -b option, broken symlinks can be detected

.TP
.B \-d
Display the crc in decimal format

.TP
.B \-e \fIexclude_file\fR
Excludes files, directories, or paths matching entries listed in
\fIexclude_file\fR. Entries can be exact matches, prefix matches
(respecting directory boundaries), simple patterns, or regular expressions
(if \fB-r\fR is enabled). Comment lines starting with \fB#\fR and blank
lines in the exclusion file are ignored.

.TP
.B \-r
Regular expression matching for exclusions. When specified alongside
\fB-e\fR, entries in the exclusion file are treated as extended regular
expressions (\fRregcomp\fR(3) / \fRregexec\fR(3)) instead of standard shell
wildcards/prefix matches using \fBfnmatch\fR(3).

.TP
.B \-f \fIlist_file\fR
Reads paths and optional per-path tags to process from an external list
file, one per line. Blank lines and lines starting with \fB#\fR are
ignored.

.TP
.B \-i \fIinitial_crc\fR
Use \fIinitial_crc\fR instead of 0 to initiaze CRC.

.TP
.B \-l
Line-by-line mode. Computes the CRC sum by evaluating each line of a text
file individually rather than the raw binary stream of the entire file.
Using -l option makes crc calculate crc for every line and print the sum of
the CRC for every line. This feature will report identical crc for files
having the same lines even if the lines are in different order within the
file. If -w option is used along with -l, leading and trailing white
spaces, CR and LF will be ignored when calculating the CRC.

.TP
.B \-w
Ignore whitespace and newline differences. Triggers line-by-line processing
(requires \fB-l\fR) and strips leading/trailing spaces, tabs, carriage
returns (\fR\\r\fR), and newlines (\fR\\n\fR) before calculating individual
line checksums.

.TP
.B \-o \fIoutput_file\fR
Writes all program output to \fIoutput_file\fR instead of standard output.
If not specified, output goes to standard output (`stdout`).

.TP
.B \-R
Recursive descend into directories specified on the command line or in
\fIlist_file\fR. If a directory starts with '-', then regular files are
recursively skipped for the directory.

.TP
.B \-t \fItag\fR
Applies the specified \fItag\fR to all paths provided directly on the
command line. When running in verbose mode (\fB-v\fR), `,tag=\fItag\fR` is
appended to the output line for each processed file or directory.

.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode. Prints file metadata (permissions, owner, group, month, day,
time, year) and file/directory paths alongside the calculated CRC-32
checksum (and tags, if provided).

.SH CRCDIFF OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fIcritical_list\fR
Directories/files listed in \fIcritical_list\fR will be used to give L2
warnings for changes to such files.

.TP
.B \-C
The -C option displays the checksum in the diff.

.TP
.B \-i
CRC change without date change will be ignored and not reported. This will
also not report entries labelled as \fBchanged\fR.

.TP
.B \-d
The -d option ignores directory timestamp changes and \fBreplaced\fR
entries for directories are omitted.

.SH EXCLUSION FILE FORMAT
The file specified via \fB-e\fR can contain paths or patterns. Each line is
evaluated as follows.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBExact Match:\fR Matches the exact path string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPrefix Match:\fR Automatically matches subdirectories and files
contained within an excluded directory path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPattern / Regex Match:\fR Uses system pattern matching (\fRfnmatch\fR on
Unix, \fRPathMatchSpecA\fR on Windows) or standard regex when \fB-r\fR is
passed.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Run in verbose mode recursively with a command-line tag and redirect output:

.B crc -vR -t os -o os_crc.txt /etc

.TP
Exclude specific directories/patterns defined in an exclude file:

.B crc -R -e exclude.txt /var/log

.TP
Use regular expressions for advanced pattern exclusions:

.B crc -Rr -e exclude_regex.txt /home/user/project

.TP
Recurse and print just directory entries

.B crc -Rr -e exclude_regex.txt /usr/lib

.SH NOTE
crcdiff(1), pkgdiff(1), portdiff(1) programs use a hash join, sometimes
called a hash-based lookup. Each program reads the second file into a hash
table keyed by the identifying field, then streams through the first file
doing one lookup per line. That replaces the old nested loop over every
pair of lines (O(n×m)) with roughly O(n+m) work. The same is done in the
opposite direction to find added entries.

.TP 4
The hash table details are the same in all three:

Hash function: djb2 (h = h*33 + c, starting at 5381).

Collision handling: open addressing with linear probing. The table size is
a power of two, at least twice the number of entries, so lookups use a bit
mask rather than a modulo.

Stored values: each slot holds an index into the array of lines rather than
a copy of the line, so the table stays small.

.TP 4
The key differs per program:

crcdiff: the filename, plus a second table keyed on the whole line without
,tag=…. The first match by line number wins.

portdiff: the first word of the line, such as 22/tcp6.

pkgdiff: the package name plus architecture, plus a table keyed on the
whole line without install_time.

.PP
pkgdiff goes one step further. Each hash slot holds a chain of all entries
with the same key, in file order. It first pairs identical lines, then
pairs the leftovers one-to-one by popping from those chains. That is how
the multi-kernel case is handled.

.SH BUGS
Altough extremely unlikely, files with different data may still produce the
same crc value.

